---
title: Shared Defaults & Java
description: Share parser, logging, update, and language defaults and call the builder from Java.
---

## Shared settings

```kotlin
import net.ririfa.yacla.LoaderSettings
import net.ririfa.yacla.Yacla
import net.ririfa.yacla.Yacla.loader
import net.ririfa.yacla.yaml.YamlParser
import java.nio.file.Paths

val settings = LoaderSettings(parser = YamlParser(), autoUpdate = true)

val config = Yacla.withDefaults(settings) {
    loader<AppConfig> {
        fromResource("/defaults/config.yml")
        toFile(Paths.get("config.yml"))
    }.config
}
```

The scope applies defaults before the per-loader block. Override settings in that block when a config needs different behavior. Shared defaults are scoped to the block and do not mutate global state.

| `LoaderSettings` property | Default |
| --- | --- |
| `parser` | Required |
| `logger` | null |
| `autoUpdate` | false |
| `languageSettings` | JVM language, `en` fallback, single file |
| `updateStrategy` | null, meaning the parser's default |

`LanguageSettings` can provide shared language, fallback, output mode, and output languages. See [Localized Defaults](/yacla/localization/).

## Logging

Pass a `YaclaLogger` to `withLogger(...)`, shortcut `logger`, or shared settings. `net.ririfa.yacla.logger.impl.SLF4JYaclaLogger` is provided for applications with SLF4J on their runtime classpath. Yacla core declares SLF4J as compile-only; your application must supply the API and a logging provider when using this adapter.

## Java entry point

Use a Kotlin-defined `AppConfig` with a primary constructor, such as the class in [Getting Started](/yacla/getting-started/).

```java
import net.ririfa.yacla.Yacla;
import net.ririfa.yacla.loader.ConfigLoader;
import net.ririfa.yacla.yaml.YamlParser;
import java.nio.file.Paths;

ConfigLoader<AppConfig> loader = Yacla.loader(AppConfig.class)
    .fromResource("/defaults/config.yml")
    .toFile(Paths.get("config.yml"))
    .parser(new YamlParser())
    .autoUpdateIfOutdated(true)
    .load();

AppConfig config = loader.getConfig();
```

Java has a functional `Yacla.withDefaults(settings, scope -> ...)` overload. Inside that scope, `scope.loader(AppConfig.class, builder -> { ... })` builds and loads the config. Ordinary Java POJOs and records do not meet the current Kotlin-primary-constructor mapping contract.
